UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 39, Lesson 2: Presenting our creative showcase', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Presenting the creative showcase means welcoming guests, managing time, performing with professional calm and thanking helpers. If something fails, use backup plans without blaming anyone publicly. Celebrate ensemble effort.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWrite a 20-second welcome script and one public recovery sentence if tech fails.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Welcome names theme and thanks; recovery is calm such as ''We’ll continue with our unplugged version.''\n\nWATCH FOR\nApologising repeatedly onstage. Recover, then reflect privately later.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPresent your showcase (or a scaled home version). Complete a gratitude list and one recovery note.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"Presenting the creative showcase means welcoming guests, managing time, performing with professional calm and thanking helpers. If something fails, use backup plans without blaming anyone publicly. Celebrate ensemble effort.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Create and perform","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-DRA-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Present your showcase (or a scaled home version). Complete a gratitude list and one recovery note. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using present on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Apologising repeatedly onstage.. Correction: Strong answer: Welcome names theme and thanks;. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Apologising repeatedly onstage. Recover, then reflect privately later. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating present on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with present, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about present with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for present. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for present. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Welcome names theme and thanks","Apologising repeatedly onstage","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material"],"question":"Write a 20-second welcome script and one public recovery sentence if tech fails. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for present; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of audience care and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Apologising repeatedly onstage","Presenting the creative showcase means welcoming guests, managing time, performing with professional calm and thanking","Welcome names theme and thanks"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with present?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named audience care and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Apologising repeatedly onstage","Name audience care and explain why it matters only, without linking it to present","Apply present to new material and name the audience care and explain why it matters used"],"question":"Which response best applies present with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on present. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Welcome names theme and thanks","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","Apologising repeatedly onstage"],"question":"Which response best uses elements, techniques and audience effect for present?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for present","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","Apologising repeatedly onstage"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about present?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Presenting the creative showcase means welcoming guests, managing time) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","Example: Host welcomes, states theme, reminds about exits/phones if needed","Apologising repeatedly onstage"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about present?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue"],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for present?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Retype the worked example with no new example","Apologising repeatedly onstage","Name audience care and explain why it matters only, with no linked example","Present your showcase (or a scaled home version)"],"question":"Which independent response best applies present to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about present with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating present on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using present on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for present. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with present, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Apologising repeatedly onstage.. Correction: Strong answer: Welcome names theme and thanks;. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y4-w39-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise present.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Creative Arts Showcase","success_criteria":["I can use present with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify audience care and explain why it matters.","I can practise gratitude independently on a new prompt.","I can notice professional calm needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Presenting the creative showcase means welcoming guests, managing time, performing with professional calm and thanking helpers.","options":["Presenting the creative showcase means welcoming guests, managing time, performing with professional calm and thanking","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about present with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Host welcomes, states theme, reminds about exits/phones if needed. After a missed cue, cast continues; thanks include display team and families.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite a 20-second welcome script and one public recovery sentence if tech fails.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Welcome names theme and thanks; recovery is calm such as ''We’ll continue with our unplugged version.''","explain":"Presenting the creative showcase means welcoming guests, managing time, performing with professional calm and thanking helpers. If something fails, use backup plans without blaming anyone publicly. Celebrate ensemble effort.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Apologising repeatedly onstage. Recover, then reflect privately later. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Presenting well.","caption":"No public blame.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Welcome</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">theme + thanks</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Recover</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">backup calmly</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Host welcomes, states theme, reminds about exits/phones if needed. After a missed cue, cast continues; thanks i Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Host welcomes, states theme, reminds about exits/phones if needed. After a missed cue, cast continues; thanks i Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. Apply the lesson idea with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about present with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Host welcomes, states theme, reminds about exits/phones if needed. After a missed cue, cast continues; thanks include display team and families. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Celebration tone.","caption":"Thank widely.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Gratitude</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">crew families</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Ensemble</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">all roles matter</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison.","options":["Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about present with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nApologising repeatedly onstage. Recover, then reflect privately later. Here is a more helpful example: Example: Host welcomes, states theme, reminds about exits/phones if needed. After a missed cue, cast continues; thanks include display team and families.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Welcome names theme and thanks; recovery is calm such as ''We’ll continue with our unplugged version.''","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration w